1
00:00:00,928 --> 00:00:09,844
Welcome to the Asia capitals, episode two of seven. First, I clear the chosen
names for a fresh regional lesson.

2
00:00:10,500 --> 00:00:20,009
Five capitals of East Asia: the northern and southern Korean states, the two
great East Asian powers, and Taipei in the north of Taiwan.

3
00:00:20,666 --> 00:00:22,756
Let's bring Beijing into view.

4
00:00:27,164 --> 00:00:36,974
Beijing lies in the north of China, on the plain where the Yongding and
Chaobai rivers meet, a short step from the Great Wall.

5
00:00:37,631 --> 00:00:49,868
"Northern capital" — bei north, jing capital; Nanjing is the southern one,
with the same second character. It has been the capital on and off since
Kublai Khan made it one in 1272.

6
00:00:50,532 --> 00:00:59,611
Remember Beijing as the northern capital, whose Forbidden City has 980
buildings and was closed to ordinary people for nearly five hundred years.

7
00:01:00,295 --> 00:01:03,441
Let's bring Pyongyang into view.

8
00:01:06,060 --> 00:01:11,900
Pyongyang stands on the Taedong river in the west of North Korea, on the plain
of the same name.

9
00:01:12,560 --> 00:01:24,205
"Flat land". Its Juche Tower, 170 m high, was finished in 1982, and only
people approved by the state are allowed to live in it.

10
00:01:24,870 --> 00:01:32,080
Remember Pyongyang as the flat-land capital with the Juche Tower, where only
the state's approved may live.

11
00:01:32,763 --> 00:01:35,143
Let's bring Seoul into view.

12
00:01:38,544 --> 00:01:47,368
Seoul stands in the middle of the Korean peninsula, on the Han river, and
about half of all South Koreans live in it or its surrounding region.

13
00:01:48,031 --> 00:02:00,013
Unusual among Korean place names in being pure native Korean rather than
Sino-Korean: seoul simply means "capital", so the city is called Capital.

14
00:02:00,670 --> 00:02:08,298
Remember Seoul as the city whose name simply means capital, rebuilt almost
entirely after the Korean War.

15
00:02:08,962 --> 00:02:11,005
Let's bring Tokyo into view.

16
00:02:14,739 --> 00:02:22,413
Tokyo stands on the eastern coast of Honshu, where the Sumida and Arakawa
rivers reach the bay of the Pacific.

17
00:02:23,078 --> 00:02:38,270
"Eastern capital", renamed from Edo in 1868 when the emperor moved here from
Kyōto, the old capital in the west. Greater Tokyo is the largest metropolitan
area in the world, about 37 million people.

18
00:02:38,933 --> 00:02:49,034
Remember Tokyo as the eastern capital, and Edo, already the largest city in
the world by 1720 with over a million people.

19
00:02:49,712 --> 00:02:51,802
Let's bring Taipei into view.

20
00:02:55,509 --> 00:03:04,379
Taipei stands in the north of Taiwan, in the basin of the Tamsui river, a
short way inland from the coast.

21
00:03:05,039 --> 00:03:15,186
"North Taiwan". Its National Palace Museum holds treasures that came from the
imperial collections of Beijing, and its night markets are among the busiest
in Asia.

22
00:03:15,844 --> 00:03:24,110
Remember Taipei as the north of Taiwan, guardian of the imperial treasures
from Beijing, with the night markets of Asia.

23
00:03:28,820 --> 00:03:42,473
Beijing is the northern capital of China, Pyongyang the flat-land capital of
North Korea, Seoul the capital of South Korea, Tokyo the eastern capital of
Japan, and Taipei the north of Taiwan.

24
00:03:43,139 --> 00:03:58,882
Remember the Forbidden City closed for five hundred years, the Juche Tower,
the city whose name simply means capital, Edo the largest city in the world by
1720, and the night markets of Taipei.
